# Copy to .env and edit. Every variable can also be read from a file by
# setting NAME_FILE=/path (Docker secrets). Never commit .env.
#
# The platforms' credentials, rates and public-only switches, and the proxy,
# can be set in Settings > Accounts instead: saved there, encrypted, they take
# precedence over these until reset. The server settings below stay here.

# --- Docker Compose -------------------------------------------------------
# Read by docker compose itself, not by HuntDeck.

# Where the UI is published on the host (default: localhost only).
# HUNTDECK_PUBLISH=127.0.0.1:8080

# The data directory: a named volume by default, or a host path such as
# ./data (make it writable by UID 65532: sudo chown -R 65532:65532 ./data).
# HUNTDECK_DATA_PATH=huntdeck-data

# --- Server ---------------------------------------------------------------

# HTTP listen address inside the container, and the data directory.
# docker compose sets these itself; the image defaults to the same values.
# Change them only when running the binary without Docker.
# HUNTDECK_LISTEN=0.0.0.0:8080
# HUNTDECK_DATA_DIR=/data

# debug | info | warn | error
HUNTDECK_LOG_LEVEL=info

# text | json
HUNTDECK_LOG_FORMAT=text

# Optional proxy for all platform traffic, e.g. Burp on your machine:
# http://host.docker.internal:8080 (127.0.0.1 inside the container is
# HuntDeck itself; on Linux, map host.docker.internal with extra_hosts).
# Every platform's requests and custom sources go through it, and so does the
# login browser (one Chrome, used by every platform login). With any platform
# login set it must be an http:// proxy (socks5:// works for API tokens
# only); it may carry credentials (http://user:password@host:port).
# Notifications and the one-time starter seed download don't use it.
# HUNTDECK_PROXY=

# HuntDeck has no accounts. IPs and localhost always work; if you open the UI by
# a hostname (e.g. a tailnet name), list it here, comma-separated.
# HUNTDECK_ALLOWED_HOSTS=

# Behind a reverse proxy (1Panel, nginx, Caddy, Cloudflare), the login budgets
# and rate limits would otherwise see the proxy's address for everyone. Name the
# header it puts the visitor's address in: X-Real-IP for nginx/1Panel,
# CF-Connecting-IP for Cloudflare. Leave it unset when nothing proxies HuntDeck.
# HUNTDECK_CLIENT_IP_HEADER=

# Ask for this password before showing anything (one password for the whole
# install; still no user accounts). Set it when the copy is on a public
# address, with TLS in front: the login page keeps a 30-day signed cookie.
# At least 8 characters; changing it signs every browser out.
# HUNTDECK_PASSWORD=

# The interface language when a visitor hasn't picked one in the sidebar:
# auto (the browser's), en, or a language with a catalog such as zh.
# HUNTDECK_LANGUAGE=auto

# Time zone dates and times are shown in (an IANA name such as
# Australia/Brisbane or UTC) until you pick one in Settings. Data is stored in UTC.
# HUNTDECK_TIMEZONE=America/New_York

# A new install loads the public starter seed (public programs' history) on
# its first start, once. "off" starts empty; or the https URL of another
# seed (its SHA-256 at the same URL plus .sha256).
# HUNTDECK_SEED_URL=off

# Platforms HuntDeck has no client for, as custom sources: name=location pairs
# (an absolute file path or an http(s) address) separated by spaces, each a JSON
# document your own script writes. See docs/custom-sources.md.
# HUNTDECK_SOURCES=inspectiv=/data/sources/inspectiv.json
# How often each is read in full (new programs every quarter of that).
# HUNTDECK_SOURCES_INTERVAL=1h

# --- HackerOne ----------------------------------------------------------------
# API username and token from https://hackerone.com/settings/api_token/edit

H1_USERNAME=
H1_TOKEN=

# Optional website login (hackerone.com), for what the API lacks: private
# programs' stats and the invitations waiting to be accepted (listed, with
# an Accept button). It runs in the login browser below, through
# HUNTDECK_PROXY, and the session is kept in the data directory.
# H1_EMAIL=
# H1_PASSWORD=
# H1_TOTP_SECRET=          # the 2FA app's secret (base32), if 2FA is on
# H1_OTP= is taken for H1_TOTP_SECRET too


# Poll intervals below are defaults: values saved in the Settings page win
# until reset there. Settings can also disable a platform entirely.
# Full poll: refresh every program's scope (~1 request per program). 0 disables.
# H1_FULL_INTERVAL=6h
# Quick poll: list programs only, fetch scope for new ones/invites. 0 disables.
# H1_QUICK_INTERVAL=15m
# Requests per second (HackerOne allows 10/s; stay well under).
# H1_RATE=5
# Parallel scope fetches.
# H1_CONCURRENCY=4

# --- Bugcrowd -----------------------------------------------------------------
# No official API: HuntDeck reads what the bugcrowd.com web app reads. Pick one:
#  1. Email + password (+ the base32 secret of your authenticator app, shown
#     when you add the app in Bugcrowd's 2FA settings). HuntDeck logs in again
#     by itself whenever the session ends. Recommended.
#  2. BC_SESSION: the _bugcrowd_session cookie from your browser. Lasts about
#     14 days; paste a new one when it expires.
#  3. BC_PUBLIC_ONLY=true with no credentials: public programs only.
# Wrap values containing $ in single quotes, e.g. BC_PASSWORD='pa$$word#1'
# (docker compose and shells treat $ as a variable otherwise).

# BC_EMAIL=
# BC_PASSWORD=
# BC_TOTP_SECRET=
# BC_SESSION=
# BC_PUBLIC_ONLY=false

# Bugcrowd's WAF blocks fast clients for hours; keep the rate at 1/s or below.
# BC_FULL_INTERVAL=6h
# BC_QUICK_INTERVAL=30m
# BC_RATE=1
# BC_CONCURRENCY=2

# --- Intigriti ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Official researcher API. Create a personal access token in Intigriti under
# your profile > Personal access tokens (copy it when it's shown; the list
# shows a different ID).

# IT_TOKEN=
# IT_FULL_INTERVAL=6h
# IT_QUICK_INTERVAL=15m
# IT_RATE=2
# IT_CONCURRENCY=2

# Optional web login, for the program stats the API lacks (hunters, response
# times, payouts, last report), and to read your own reports (Reports,
# Analytics). The first login runs a real, windowed Chrome
# (on a virtual display, inside the container); after that the session renews
# itself. Its login page scores visitors with reCAPTCHA, so set HUNTDECK_PROXY
# to a residential http:// proxy with a good reputation. In .env files, put
# values containing # or $ in single quotes: 'pa#ss$word'.
# IT_EMAIL=
# IT_PASSWORD=
# IT_TOTP_SECRET=          # the 2FA app's secret (base32), if 2FA is on

# The login browser. It's found on PATH (the image includes Google Chrome,
# or Chromium when built for arm64).
# Its clock should match the proxy's country (default: HUNTDECK_TIMEZONE).
# HUNTDECK_CHROME=
# HUNTDECK_CHROME_TIMEZONE=America/New_York
# HUNTDECK_CHROME_HEADLESS=false
# Website logins share one Chrome, a tab per platform. HUNTDECK_VNC shows it
# live in Settings > Browser (and at /novnc/), to watch a login, look at a
# platform's session, or finish a login by hand if a site asks for a person.
# HUNTDECK_VNC=false

# --- YesWeHack ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Email + password (+ the base32 secret of your authenticator app if 2FA is
# on) to see private invites; HuntDeck logs in again when its token expires
# (every 4 hours). YWH_TOKEN takes a bearer token from your browser instead,
# but those expire within hours. YWH_PUBLIC_ONLY=true polls public programs
# without an account.

# YWH_EMAIL=
# YWH_PASSWORD=
# YWH_TOTP_SECRET=
# YWH_TOKEN=
# YWH_PUBLIC_ONLY=false
# YWH_FULL_INTERVAL=6h
# YWH_QUICK_INTERVAL=30m
# YWH_RATE=2
# YWH_CONCURRENCY=2

# Where a read-only copy is published (the UI then adds canonical links, a
# sitemap and link previews and allows search engines); a full install is
# never indexed, so leave this unset for one.
# HUNTDECK_PUBLIC_URL=

# The UI's "Send feedback" links mail here (unset hides them), the news link
# in a read-only copy's footer (an X account, e.g. huntdeck), and the source
# repository it links to (a fork may point home).
# Optional AI: an OpenAI-compatible endpoint. Set both or neither; with neither,
# every page works exactly as it does without AI. `huntdeck ai-check` verifies them.
# HUNTDECK_AI_BASE_URL=https://api.deepseek.com/v1
# HUNTDECK_AI_MODEL=deepseek-chat
# HUNTDECK_AI_KEY=

# HUNTDECK_FEEDBACK_EMAIL=
# HUNTDECK_X_ACCOUNT=
# HUNTDECK_REPO_URL=
# POJIA (cve.pojia.ai) indexes disclosed bug bounty reports; a key adds the
# what-it-has-paid-for panel to program pages.
#HUNTDECK_POJIA_KEY=
#HUNTDECK_POJIA_URL=https://cve.pojia.ai/mcp
# A ScopeSentry instance: HuntDeck hands it the targets it finds, as scan tasks.
# The key is an ssk_ key scoped to task:write, task:read, node:read, template:read.
#HUNTDECK_SCAN_URL=http://10.0.8.4:8082
#HUNTDECK_SCAN_KEY=
# How many tasks one read-back pass asks the scanner about (default 8; its MCP
# allows about ten calls a minute).
#HUNTDECK_SCAN_READ_BATCH=8
# typesafe.ai's Jev: a typed, calibrated judgement asked before a scan handoff.
# A block or confirm answer stands the handoff down. Advisory: the memory
# floor and the other deterministic checks run first.
#HUNTDECK_JEV_KEY=
#HUNTDECK_JEV_MODEL=jev-latest
# Which decision service answers: typesafe (default), bailian, cloudflare or
# openai. The first three speak the same protocol; only the address and the
# credentials differ. OpenAI uses its Decisions API (gpt-6-luna).
#HUNTDECK_DECISION_PROVIDER=typesafe
# Only for the cloudflare provider: the account whose Workers AI runs clef-flash.
#HUNTDECK_CF_ACCOUNT_ID=
# Only for the bailian provider: the workspace id sent with each request.
#HUNTDECK_BAILIAN_WORKSPACE_ID=
#HUNTDECK_JEV_URL=https://api.typesafe.ai
# An HTTP proxy the call leaves through when the host's own egress is blocked.
#HUNTDECK_JEV_PROXY=http://172.17.0.1:17890
# --- HuntMail -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Your HuntMail inbox (https://github.com/mdpsec/huntmail), a catch-all on your
# own Cloudflare account, for the Email page and each program's Email tab: the
# address with its base path and the API key, as HuntMail's setup printed them.
# HUNTMAIL_DOMAINS: domains for new addresses (default: HuntMail's own list).
# Or leave these empty and use Email › HuntMail › Setup: HuntDeck
# puts HuntMail on your Cloudflare account and saves them for you.

# HUNTMAIL_URL=
# HUNTMAIL_API_KEY=
# HUNTMAIL_DOMAINS=
